//@version=6
indicator("Vishal's Upper Pane", shorttitle="Upper Pane", overlay=true, max_lines_count=500, max_labels_count=500, max_bars_back=500)

// ============================================================================
//  VISHAL'S UPPER PANE  (formerly "The 200 Line — Trend Deck")
//  An all-in-one overlay: Ripster EMA clouds + MTF clouds, daily SMA/WMA lines,
//  a 2nd-timeframe MTF SMA set, secular 200W/300W/200M lines, auto fib levels
//  (0.618 / 1.618, swing-anchored, line-drawn = zero plot cost), SATY ATR
//  LEVELS (the 0DTE day map: pivot, triggers, targets, extensions), the 200
//  Line trend-regime engine (buffer band + slope gate, IN/OUT signals), daily
//  50/200 golden / death crosses, an RSI readout, an ADR PULSE readout
//  (retracement from this week's high measured in ADR multiples), and a 0DTE
//  Regime Score table plus an 11-event 0DTE alert layer built on alert() (zero
//  plot cost). Each block has its own master checkbox.
//
//  v6.32 changes:
//   • REGIME GATE DEFAULT is now TVC:VIX, and the input was RENAMED so the new
//     default actually reaches charts that already saved the old value (a new
//     title is a new key — the v6.21 lesson, applied deliberately this time).
//     Reason, verified in-browser on a paid plan 2026-07-27: CBOE:VIX1D is
//     DELAYED BY 15 MINUTES, and TradingView will not save an alert whose script
//     touches a delayed symbol. The error text blames a missing subscription,
//     which sends you looking in the wrong place, because the chart opens fine.
//     SPY, QQQ and TVC:US10Y were all confirmed real-time, so this was the only
//     blocker. VIX1D remains the validated gate; this is an entitlement
//     compromise on the CHART only — the Telegram watcher still uses VIX1D.
//
//  v6.31 changes:
//   • The exhaustion level was there but nobody could see it: 1px, in a colour
//     close to the 1/2/3 marks, drawn inside a pale cloud. It now has its OWN
//     colour input (bright pink by default) and 2px width, and its right-edge
//     tag is dashed so it reads as conditional rather than as a standing level.
//     Visibility is not cosmetic here — this line is INTERMITTENT by nature,
//     appearing only while an episode is armed, so unlike the contours it cannot
//     rely on always being in the same place to be found.
//
//  v6.30 changes:
//   • THE SCORE CLOUD — the band between the two contours is now shaded, and it
//     is not decoration. That band is exactly the region in which the score sits
//     between -8.5 and +8.5, so PRICE LEAVING THE CLOUD IS THE THRESHOLD EVENT.
//     Above the cloud the score has broken +8.5; below it, -8.5. Reading a
//     stretch print becomes "price is outside the band" instead of comparing two
//     numbers, and it needs no scale, no label and no arithmetic at a glance.
//   • SECOND READING, free with the first: the band's WIDTH is how many points
//     of price separate the two thresholds — i.e. how sensitive the score is to
//     price right now. A narrow cloud means a small move swings the score a long
//     way; a wide one means the tape has room before anything fires.
//   • 1 fill slot, not 2: the colour is input-qualified rather than series. A
//     fill that recoloured on exit would cost double, and the exit is already
//     obvious — the candles are outside the shading.
//
//  v6.29 changes:
//   • EXHAUSTION-FIRE LEVEL added — the price at which the 1/2/3 mark would
//     print, i.e. where the score recovers zdRevDelta from the episode extreme.
//     Same isoline solver, aimed at a MOVING target (extreme + delta) instead of
//     a fixed threshold, so it exists only while an episode is armed. The two
//     sides are mutually exclusive, so one plot covers both. 58/64.
//   • LABELLED AS A MARKER, NOT AN ENTRY, in the input tooltip and on the chart
//     label itself, because the project has measured and rejected the reversal
//     trade three separate ways: REVERSAL_STUDY_REPORT.md (dead unconditionally,
//     N=3,307, 30m mean +0.0 bps), the exhaustion finding (extreme MARKER at ~2x
//     no-skill, explicitly not an entry) and ATR_STRETCH_REPORT.md (~2 points,
//     26% of the noise). A precise trigger for a dead edge is worse than none.
//   • The RSI confirm is a SEPARATE gate and is not in the price, so the readout
//     states whether it currently confirms. A level that cannot fire should not
//     look like one that can.
//
//  v6.28 changes — THE LINE HUGGED PRICE:
//   • The level sat far too close to price, so it "rode" the threshold instead of
//     being approached and tapped. Cause: v6.27 solved the four-leg AVERAGE by
//     LINEARISING each leg — take its isoline, read a score-per-price slope, sum
//     them. Every leg SATURATES (rsiSig clamps at ±1, the leg score at ±10), but
//     a linear slope has no cap, so a fast 2-minute leg kept contributing long
//     past the point it could move at all. Denominator too big -> required move
//     too small -> line too close. It erred the same direction every time.
//     Measured against an exact solve on the decade cache: the true level is
//     1.4x-1.5x FURTHER from price.
//   • Fixed by removing the approximation rather than tuning it. The four legs
//     now export their STATE through the request.security calls they were
//     already making, and the true composite average is bisected directly on the
//     chart timeframe. Cheaper too: one composite solve per bar instead of eight
//     per-leg solves. The score formula now lives in ONE function used both for
//     the live score and for every trial price, so they cannot drift apart.
//   • NEW input: which score contour to plot (default 8.5 = the C/3 threshold,
//     so the lines correspond to the 1/2/3 marks). Raising it to 9.5 moves the
//     lines away from price but nothing in the deck fires at 9.5.
//
//  v6.27 changes — THE GAPS:
//   • The line went blank for ~an hour on hard sell-off days. Cause: v6.26 only
//     ever looked for the level BELOW price, so once the score was already past
//     -8.5 there was nothing to find and the line stopped. Measured on 2026-06-09
//     and 06-05, every single gap minute had a median score of -9.2 to -9.5.
//   • The premise was wrong. This is an ISOLINE — the contour where the score
//     equals the threshold — not a floor under price. Above it, it sits below
//     you and says where the score breaks. Inside the zone, it sits ABOVE you and
//     says where the score climbs back out. Same contour, and the score is
//     monotone in price, so there is exactly one crossing either way.
//     Each leg now brackets the FULL +/-5% band and the composite keeps only the
//     band guard. A positive % on the -8.5 row means you are already in the zone.
//   • 16 bisections over a 10% band still resolves to ~0.01 of a point at 7400.
//
//  v6.26 changes — THE SPIKES:
//   • The plotted levels threw vertical spikes of 100-200 points. Two causes,
//     both in the composite step, neither visible until the level was drawn as a
//     line instead of a single number at the right edge. Plotting it was what
//     exposed it.
//     (1) SIGN-FLIPPED SLOPES. A leg already past the threshold produces a
//         NEGATIVE score-vs-price slope. Summed with healthy legs it cancelled
//         them, drove the denominator toward zero and hurled the level away.
//         f_slope now returns na unless the slope is positive — one test that
//         covers both sides, since the score rises with price by construction.
//     (2) UNBOUNDED EXTRAPOLATION. Each leg is solved inside a +/-5% band, so a
//         composite landing outside that band is extrapolation past anything
//         actually evaluated. Those now return na, and the line breaks, which is
//         the honest rendering of "no answer" — style_linebr was already there.
//   • The old guard only tested slope == 0 exactly, so near-zero sailed through.
//     Now: slope sum must be positive AND at least two legs must contribute.
//
//  v6.25 changes:
//   • THE TWO SCORE-TO-PRICE LEVELS ARE NOW CONTINUOUS LINES, running across the
//     chart like a moving average instead of appearing only at the right edge.
//     You can now see how the trigger moved through the session and how close
//     price actually came to it. Costs 2 plot slots — the only plot cost in the
//     entire 0DTE half; everything else there is labels, lines and tables.
//   • MADE AFFORDABLE by bounding the WINDOW rather than the bar. v6.24 gated the
//     solver on barstate.islast because bisecting on every historical bar is
//     ~100M float ops over a decade and trips RE10140. Three changes buy the
//     history back: the solver now runs only over the last N bars (default 500,
//     a bit over one 1-minute session), the four LONGER-TF legs skip it entirely
//     via a new f_zdScore(_solve) argument since their levels were never used,
//     and the bisection is 16 evaluations instead of 21 — which still resolves a
//     5% band to ~0.01 of a point at SPX 7400.
//     Net: ~64,000 evaluations instead of ~100,000,000.
//   • NOTE this does NOT help with alerts. An alert referencing an indicator plot
//     is a TECHNICAL alert and a Basic plan has zero of those — verified against
//     the paywall. The plotted line is for READING; the free alert still has to
//     be a typed Value from the PRICE ALERT AT row.
//
//  v6.24 changes:
//   • THE SCORE-TO-PRICE LEVEL IS NOW EXACT, not an RSI-only estimate. v6.23
//     inverted Wilder RSI in closed form, which forced it to freeze the EMA
//     stack, price-vs-EMA26 and MACD at their aligned values — those are step
//     functions and have no analytic inverse. Replaced with BISECTION on the
//     real score function: every component is re-evaluated at the trial price
//     exactly as the live score computes it. Works because the score is monotone
//     in price. 20 iterations resolves a 5% band to well under a tick.
//     Only two approximations remain and both are small: VWAP is held (weight
//     0.15 of ~10, and a session accumulation barely moves in one bar), and it
//     solves for one next-bar close rather than a path.
//   • The solver is gated on barstate.islast, so it runs once per refresh rather
//     than on every historical bar. Ungated this would be ~100M float ops over a
//     decade of 1-minute bars and would trip Pine's execution limit.
//
//  v6.23 changes:
//   • SCORE-TO-PRICE LEVELS, so a FREE TradingView plan can alert on the 1/2/3.
//     Basic allows 0 technical alerts — an indicator can never raise one — but
//     it does allow PRICE alerts. Wilder RSI is invertible, and RSI carries ~71%
//     of this score's weight, so the price that would put the composite on the
//     tier-3 threshold is computable. Drawn as a dotted line per side plus a
//     readable number in the new PRICE ALERT AT row; set a free price alert
//     there. Costs ZERO plot slots and ZERO extra request.security calls — the
//     maths rides inside the four score calls that already run, by widening
//     their return tuple. Two approximations, both documented at the input.
//
//  v6.22 changes — THE FIX v6.21 COULD NOT DELIVER:
//   • v6.21 flipped the 0DTE alert master to default ON and correctly warned
//     that an already-added copy would keep its saved value. That warning was
//     the whole problem: a default can NEVER reach a chart that has already
//     saved the opposite, so on every existing chart the marks kept drawing and
//     nothing kept being sent. A source update cannot fix a saved input.
//     THE MASTER SWITCH IS THEREFORE GONE. Alerts are unconditional, and the
//     only control is "MUTE all 0DTE alerts" — a NEW input, which by definition
//     has no saved value and so does take its default (false = not muted). The
//     three sub-toggles were renamed for the same reason: a new name is a new
//     key, and a new key picks up its default.
//   • NEW "ALERTS" ROW in the 0DTE context readout, naming exactly which
//     families are live — green when something will be sent, red when nothing
//     will. This class of bug (silent misconfiguration) has now cost two
//     versions; the chart should say what it is doing rather than go quiet.
//   • NEW opt-in "Allow marks outside 09:30-16:00 ET (UNVALIDATED)". The
//     exhaustion machines are gated on the New York session, so on a 24h symbol
//     the 1/2/3 marks could not fire overnight no matter what data the chart
//     carried. This lifts that gate. Default OFF and labelled unvalidated on
//     purpose: every study behind these marks was measured RTH-only, and an
//     overnight fire is a signal wearing a validated marker's clothes.
//
//  v6.21 changes:
//   • THE 0DTE ALERTS WERE OFF BY DEFAULT AND THE CHECKBOX DID NOT SAY SO. It
//     read "alert() on zone entries and the flag" while in fact gating the
//     EXHAUSTION alerts as well, and it shipped unticked. Net effect: the
//     1 / 2 / 3 marks drew on the chart (their own switch defaults ON) and
//     nothing ever left the script. Renamed to "alert() on the 0DTE marks",
//     defaulted ON, and split into three sub-toggles — zone A/B/C (off, it is
//     the odometer and it fires often), exhaustion 1/2/3 (on), V-BOUNCE (on) —
//     so the prints worth waking up for are not buried by the ones that are not.
//   • NOTE FOR AN ALREADY-ADDED COPY: changing a default does not touch settings
//     TradingView has already saved for a chart. The master switch has to be
//     ticked by hand once per chart. The three new sub-toggles, having no saved
//     value, do pick up their new defaults.   ← superseded by v6.22
//
//  v6.20 changes:
//   • RSI now rides along as a third row inside the top-right 200 LINE / VOL
//     DIAL box (new default). Bottom left is where TradingView parks its own
//     logo, so a readout sitting there is permanently half-covered. Untick
//     "Ride along in the top-right box" to put it back on its own table.
//   • That box writes its rows through a counter now instead of fixed indices,
//     so switching the vol dial off leaves the RSI flush at the top rather than
//     floating below two empty rows. table.clear() on each redraw is what keeps
//     a switched-off row from leaving its old cells behind.
//   • Corner furniture, for reference when placing the six readouts: TradingView
//     draws its LOGO over bottom left and the CHART LEGEND over top left. The
//     four remaining positions are clean.
//
//  v6.19 changes:
//   • VALUATION & RATES readout added (table-only, 0 plot slots, 2 request
//     calls). Trailing P/E comes straight from TradingView's fundamentals feed
//     and is exact — on single stocks. It is blank on SPY / QQQ / SOXL and every
//     other ETF or index, which have no earnings of their own; the row says so
//     rather than showing a zero.
//   • Forward P/E and PEG are NOT automatic and cannot be. Pine has no
//     analyst-estimate feed, and deriving growth from reported EPS history fails
//     on a 1-minute chart because the loaded range never spans a year. Both rows
//     take a typed number — next-12m consensus EPS, and expected growth %/yr —
//     and then track price live from there. Settings are saved per chart layout,
//     so a per-ticker layout keeps its own pair of numbers.
//   • US 10-year yield row (TVC:US10Y, quoted in percent) with today's move in
//     basis points, coloured once the move clears a threshold. This is the one
//     row in the block with anything to say about a 0DTE session.
//
//  v6.18 changes:
//   • Zone marks are now LETTERS A / B / C and exhaustion marks are DIGITS
//     1 / 2 / 3. Colour and shape already separated the two families; a letter
//     against a digit separates them in the one channel that survives a
//     screenshot, a colourblind eye and a glance from across the room.
//     A/1 is the shallowest tier, C/3 the deepest. Zone alerts speak in letters
//     to match. The three threshold inputs are labelled "A / 1" and so on
//     because a single threshold drives both families at that tier.
//
//  v6.17 changes:
//   • THE CIRCLE COULD NEVER HOLD TEXT. Pine's geometric label styles — circle,
//     square, diamond, triangle — render the shape and silently drop the string.
//     Z now uses a pointerless chip, which still reads as a different silhouette
//     from the exhaustion pointer tag and does show its rank.
//   • THE DIAMOND WASN'T BROKEN, IT WAS STARVED. The climax was measured on SPY
//     volume whatever you were charting — a hangover from a system built for
//     SPX, which has no volume of its own. On TSLA or SOXL that made the
//     amplifier statistically unrelated to the bar it was supposed to amplify,
//     so it almost never coincided with a fire. It now uses the chart symbol's
//     own volume, falling back to a reference symbol only when there is none.
//   • The diamond is also a label now, pinned just beyond the E tag it modifies
//     instead of at the bar, where it sat far from its own marker. Two plot
//     slots back, and it takes the marker size control.
//
//  v6.16 changes:
//   • Z / E markers get a size control, defaulting to Normal. The tier digit is
//     drawn inside the circle, so the circle has to be large enough to hold it —
//     at the previous fixed Small the text had nowhere to sit.
//   • The amplifier diamond steps up from tiny to small. Its size is hardcoded
//     rather than exposed: plotshape() takes a const size, so it cannot be
//     driven from an input the way a label's can.
//
//  v6.15 changes:
//   • Z AND E NOW DIFFER BY COLOUR, not just by shape — Z blue, E magenta, both
//     configurable. Colour previously encoded the SIDE, which forced Z and E to
//     share a palette and left a single letter doing all the work of telling
//     them apart. Position above or below the bar already says which side, so
//     colour was free for the distinction that was actually ambiguous.
//   • Tier is no longer shaded. The digit says 1, 2 or 3; three shades across
//     two families would have been four more colours on a busy chart. Text
//     colour is picked automatically from the fill's luminance, so a light
//     custom colour still reads.
//
//  v6.14 changes:
//   • TRUST STRIP REMOVED. An overlay can only anchor to prices, so any strip
//     has to ride a price series and inherits its steps — against the pane's
//     fixed −10 axis it was a straight line, here it never could be. The WINDOW
//     row in the readout carries the same information without the pretence.
//   • MARKER STAND-OFF WAS IN THE WRONG UNIT. It scaled by the DAILY ATR while
//     placing labels on 1-minute bars: on SOXL, daily ATR near 30 points, every
//     marker sat ~10 points away from the candle it belonged to. Now measured in
//     CHART-timeframe ATRs, so it means the same thing on any timeframe.
//     ⚠ Old saved values are in the old unit — hit Reset settings after loading.
//   • Z AND E NOW LOOK DIFFERENT, on the diamond's own logic — shape carries the
//     kind, colour family the side, shade and number the tier:
//       Z  pale CIRCLE, dark text  — entering the stretch (odometer)
//       E  solid TAG, white text   — the stretch running out of road
//       ◆  orange diamond          — that fire carries an amplifier
//     Before, both were the same tag in the same teal.
//
//  v6.13 changes:
//   • CLIMAX is the orange diamond again, and only that. It marks an EXHAUSTION
//     fire that carries an amplifier — a volume climax OR a price/score
//     divergence — which is the only form the effect was ever measured in. The
//     standalone climax marks are gone entirely; a climax on its own is not the
//     signal, the pairing is. DIVERGENCE is now ported too (it was half of the
//     diamond's condition and was missing).
//   • Marker text cut to Z1/Z2/Z3 and E1/E2/E3.
//   • TRUST STRIP now rides under the SESSION LOW tracked from chart bars.
//     It was anchored to the daily request, and on historical bars a
//     higher-timeframe request returns the last COMPLETED daily bar — so the
//     strip sat at YESTERDAY'S low all session and then dropped to today's at
//     the right edge. That vertical drop was the bug, not a feature.
//     Note the same lag is harmless for the "MOVED % of a day" readout, which
//     only ever renders on the last bar, where the daily bar is the live one.
//   • V-BOUNCE no longer drops a label on the chart — it lives in the FLAG row.
//
//  v6.12 changes:
//   • TRUST WINDOW is a strip again, not a full-pane wash — one coloured bar
//     riding below the session low, offset in ATRs, broken outside the session.
//     Same look the companion pane had at the bottom of its own scale.
//   • EXHAUSTION E1/E2/E3 added. v6.11 shipped only the zone entries, which is
//     half the tiering and the less useful half: a zone entry says the stretch
