//     ATR values are still there, each behind a checkbox, default off.
//   • The box now speaks PLAIN ENGLISH by default — "GOING NOWHERE", "FELL 1.6
//     normal days", "MOVED 78% of a day" — instead of naming formulas at you.
//     ADR and ATR are both just "how far this thing normally travels in a day",
//     so the box says that and drops the acronyms. Untick "Plain-English
//     wording" in the Pulse Readout group for the old trader shorthand; the
//     numbers are identical either way.
//
//  v6.4 changes:
//   • REMOVED the volume block (scaled bars, surge marker, RVOL readout) — not
//     earning its space, and it was the most expensive thing on the chart at
//     ~6 of the 64 plot counts.
//   • NEW SATY ATR LEVELS block, built for 0DTE. Drawn with lines and labels,
//     so despite being thirteen levels it costs ZERO plot slots. One extra
//     request.security call.
//
//  v6.3 changes:
//   • Renamed to "Vishal's Upper Pane".
//   • RSI readout now FOLLOWS THE CHART TIMEFRAME by default and prints the
//     timeframe it is actually using, so it can never look "stuck" again.
//     (Old behaviour is still available: untick "Follow the chart timeframe".)
//   • NEW ADR PULSE block — ADR(20), this week's high, and RET vs ADR: how many
//     average daily ranges price has given back from the weekly high. Rows go
//     magenta past your "interesting" level (1.5x by default). Table + alert,
//     zero plot slots, one extra request.security call.
//   • The four remaining alertcondition() calls (RSI overbought/oversold, 200
//     Line IN/OUT) became alert() calls to free plot slots.
//     CONSEQUENCE: they no longer appear as named conditions in TradingView's
//     alert dropdown. EVERY alert in this script now routes through a single
//     alert on the indicator — condition "Any alert() function call", trigger
//     "Once Per Bar Close" — and each event has its own on/off checkbox in the
//     settings, so one alert covers the whole deck.
//
//  READOUT LAYOUT — four tables, one per screen corner (TradingView allows only
//  one table per position, so two in the same corner overlap):
//    Top Center    PULSE — momentum / stretch / used
//    Top Right     200 LINE / VOL DIAL
//    Bottom Left   RSI
//    Bottom Center 0DTE CONTEXT — score / window / LTF / flag / alerts (1-min)
//    Bottom Right  0DTE grid                                    (1-min only)
//  Top Left is left empty on purpose — the chart legend is drawn there.
//
//  PLOT BUDGET — the binding constraint on this script. TradingView caps a
//  script at 64 plot counts and does NOT fail compilation when you exceed it:
//  it adds the indicator, draws nothing, and raises runtime error RE10140,
//  which states the exact count. Hover the warning triangle in the chart legend
//  to read it. That number is the only reliable source — this deck measured 68
//  when a volume block was briefly added, against an estimate of 60.
//  Weighting: plot()=1 (2 with a series colour), plotcandle()=4 (+1 per
//  series-coloured argument), plotshape/plotchar=1 (more with series
//  colour/textcolor), fill()=1 (2 with a series colour), bgcolor=1,
//  alertcondition()=1. Tables, labels, lines and alert() cost NOTHING — which
//  is why the fib levels, the Saty levels and every readout are drawn that way.
//  Now ~59/64 (v6.25 +2 score-to-price lines, v6.29 +1 exhaustion level,
//  v6.30 +1 the cloud fill) and
//  request.security 25/40, so there is still room.
