// ─── ADR PULSE (Average Daily Range + retracement from the weekly high) ──────
// "One of the quickest ways to identify oversold stocks is to compare their ADR
// with the retracement from the weekly high" — the Deepvue "Ret vs ADR" column.
//
//   ADR%  = 100 × (SMA(high/low, N) − 1)  over the last N COMPLETED days
//           (Stockbee / Qullamaggie convention — today's unfinished range is
//           excluded so the yardstick doesn't move around during the session)
//   ADR$  = ADR% × price ÷ 100        (or the raw SMA(high−low, N) if you pick
//                                      the "Points" basis)
//   RET   = (anchor high − price) ÷ ADR$   where the anchor is the highest high
//           of the last N sessions (default), or this week's high if you prefer
//           the scanner convention
//
// RET of 4.26 means price has given back 4.26 average daily ranges from the
// weekly high — i.e. a week's worth of downside in one direction. The scan the
// screenshots come from filters for RET > 1.5, which is the default threshold
// here; rows past it turn magenta. This is a STRETCH reading, not a signal:
// stretched can always get more stretched, and it says nothing about trend.
// Pair it with the 200 Line / regime blocks before acting on it.
//
// The week's high is accumulated from DAILY bars inside the request.security
// context (so it is session-consistent and correct on history, not just live),
// and it re-anchors every Monday. Table + alert only — zero plot slots, one
// request.security call.
