// v6.23/24 — SCORE-TO-PRICE. A free TradingView plan has 0 technical alerts, so
// an indicator can never raise one; a PRICE alert it can. This inverts the score:
// the approximate price at which the composite would reach the tier-3 threshold.
// Set a free price alert there and you get the 1/2/3 without a paid plan.
// HOW IT IS DERIVED, and therefore what it is worth:
//   RSI carries ~71% of the composite's weight, and Wilder RSI is invertible —
//   given the current average gain/loss, the price that produces a target RSI at
//   the next close is closed-form. Per timeframe we solve for the RSI needed to
//   hit the threshold, convert that to a price, read off the score-vs-price
//   slope, then solve the 4-leg average linearly for one composite level.
//   v6.24 replaced the closed-form inversion with BISECTION on the real score,
//   so every component is now re-evaluated at the trial price. What remains:
//   (a) VWAP is held — smallest weight (0.15), and a session accumulation hardly
//       moves in one bar.
//   (b) it solves for one next-bar close, not a path, and the higher-TF legs
//       re-anchor on their own boundaries, so the level MOVES between bars.
//       It is a place to put an alert, not a fixed trigger.
zdPxOn     = input.bool(true, "Score-to-price levels (for free price alerts)", group=group0dteX, tooltip="Draws the approximate price at which the composite score would reach the tier-3 threshold on each side, as a line plus a label you can read a number off. Costs no plot slots and no extra request.security calls — the maths rides inside the score calls that already run. v6.24 solves the real score by bisection rather than inverting RSI alone, so the EMA stack, price-vs-EMA26 and MACD are all re-evaluated at the trial price. Treat the number as a place to put an alert, not a mechanical trigger — it moves as the higher-timeframe legs re-anchor.")
zdPxCol    = input.color(#00838F, "Level colour", group=group0dteX, inline="pxc")
zdExhPxCol = input.color(#FF3D71, "Exhaustion level", group=group0dteX, inline="pxc", tooltip="Deliberately brighter than the threshold contours and thicker on the chart. It is INTERMITTENT by nature — it exists only while an episode is armed — so unlike a continuous level it has to announce itself or it gets missed inside the cloud.")
zdPxFill   = input.bool(true, "…shade between them (the score cloud)", group=group0dteX, tooltip="Fills the band between the two contours. Worth more than decoration: the band IS the region where the score sits between the thresholds, so PRICE LEAVING THE CLOUD is the +/-8.5 event itself — no number-reading needed. Above the cloud = the score has broken +8.5, below it = broken -8.5. The band's WIDTH is a second reading: narrow means the score is highly sensitive to price right now (a small move swings it a long way), wide means it is insensitive and the tape has room. Costs 1 fill slot.")
zdPxLine   = input.bool(true, "…draw them as continuous lines", group=group0dteX, tooltip="Plots the two levels as real series, so they run across the chart like a moving average instead of appearing only at the right edge. Lets you see how the trigger moved through the session and how close price came to it. Costs 2 plot slots (the only plot cost in the whole 0DTE half) and is bounded by the history window below.")
